<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin-bottom: 10px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # A Dream Trip Around the World – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Exploring Our Global Community</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> A Dream Trip Around the World</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write formal and informal letters on general topics. Interactive: Write different types of messages to coordinate group projects.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a formal letter requesting information about a cultural exchange or travel program (e.g., "I am writing to inquire about the requirements for your cultural travel program. Could you please provide information on the application process and schedule?") Can write messages to coordinate logistics for a group project focused on cultural exploration (e.g., "Hi everyone, just confirming that we'll meet at 9 a.m. on Saturday to discuss travel details. Please let me know if you have any specific preferences for the trip.") Can write informal letters sharing cultural experiences with a friend (e.g., "Hey! I had an amazing time in Emberá-Wounaan. I learned so much about their traditional jewelry.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; background-color: #f2f2f2;"><h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Stage & Description</h4></th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 45%; background-color: #f2f2f2;"><h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Activities & Teacher Actions</h4></th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; background-color: #f2f2f2;"><h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Estimated Date and Time</h4></th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activates prior knowledge and introduces the lesson's context through initial interaction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying images of famous global landmarks (e.g., pyramids, waterfalls, breathtaking beaches) and asks students to briefly write down a dream destination they would visit and one unforgettable experience they would seek. Students write 2-3 sentences using the second conditional, e.g., "If I could travel anywhere, I would visit the Amazon rainforest because I would enjoy exploring its diverse nature."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models writing a quick informal message on the board to a friend, inviting them to discuss a travel plan. For instance: "Hey [Friend's Name]! Just dreaming about our next adventure. If we could go anywhere, where would you choose? I would love to explore a remote island. Let's meet soon to discuss! 👋" Teacher explicitly points out the use of informal language and the second conditional. The teacher also models the pronunciation of "could" and "would" with vowel reduction (/kəd/, /wəd/) and stress on multisyllabic words like "adVENture."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this message formal or informal?" (Informal)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who am I writing this message to?" (A friend)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What grammatical structure did I use to talk about a hypothetical trip?" (Second Conditional)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">__/__ - __/__ <br> 0-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to the scenario. Focuses on presenting target grammar, vocabulary, and discourse features.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario:</b> The teacher presents two short written texts: one a formal letter of inquiry about a "Cultural Exchange Program" (e.g., a "year-long Exchange Program" or one offering "enriching cultural experiences") and the other a set of informal WhatsApp messages coordinating a group project on "cultural exploration." These examples showcase formal/informal register, target grammar (second conditional, superlatives, past continuous), and vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Genre: Formal inquiry letter, informal chat messages. Register: Formal and informal. Format: Printed handouts or projected texts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Writing: Productive: Write formal and informal letters on general topics. Interactive: Write different types of messages to coordinate group projects.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read both texts and answer gist questions: "What is the purpose of the formal letter?" and "Who are the chat messages between and what are they planning?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students identify and underline all instances of second conditional, superlative adjectives, and past continuous verbs in both texts. They also circle at least five target vocabulary words (e.g., <i>diversity, adventure, destination, benefits, explore, learn, breathtaking, cultural</i>).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">__/__ - __/__ <br> 8-15 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing. Students practice new language in controlled and semi-controlled activities to build confidence.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing sentences and paragraphs using second conditional for hypothetical travel experiences, superlatives for comparing places, and past continuous to narrate past experiences. Incorporating target vocabulary: <i>travel, culture, experience, diversity, adventure, destination, trip, pyramids, beaches, mountains, world, waterfall, wonders, understanding, perspectives, skills, benefits, Exchange Program, traditions, explore, compare, travel, visit, experience, learn, enjoy, hike, encounter, enhance, develop, meet, understand, broaden, narrate, dream, reflect, gain, discover, share, breathtaking, global, romantic, natural, diverse, cultural, beautiful, unforgettable, personal, year-long, enriching, different, unique, educational, stunning, exciting, magnificent, remote, distant, traditional</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Cloze & Sentence Construction):</b> Students complete cloze sentences to form formal and informal expressions related to travel planning and cultural sharing. They fill in blanks with appropriate vocabulary (e.g., "I am writing to ___ about your cultural ___ program") or conjugate verbs to form second conditional, superlatives, or past continuous sentences (e.g., "If I ___ (have) enough money, I ___ (visit) the most ___ (beautiful) beaches in the world.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled - Sentence Expansion):</b> Students are given simple sentences about travel and cultural exploration and asked to expand them into more complex sentences or short paragraphs (2-3 sentences), incorporating at least one second conditional, one superlative, and one past continuous phrase. For example, "I went to a new city." -> "While traveling in the new city, I was learning about different traditions. It was the most enriching experience if I could choose."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Work - Peer Sentence Check):</b> In pairs, students exchange their expanded sentences from Activity 2. They check each other's work for correct use of target grammar and vocabulary, providing constructive feedback on clarity and accuracy. They practice respectfully discussing cultural differences and showing interest in their classmates' perspectives, for instance, by asking, "What is one tradition you found interesting while you were traveling?" or "How was your experience abroad in regards to changing your perspective?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">__/__ - __/__ <br> 16-30 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario. Focuses on fluency, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> Students will choose one of the following writing tasks, directly linked to the scenario "Exploring Our Global Community" and theme "A Dream Trip Around the World," applying the learned grammar and vocabulary:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Option A (Formal Letter):</b> Write a formal letter (150-200 words) requesting information about a cultural exchange or travel program. The letter should inquire about requirements, application processes, and benefits (e.g., "enhance skills," "broaden perspectives"). Students must include at least one second conditional sentence and two superlative adjectives.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Option B (Informal Letter):</b> Write an informal letter (150-200 words) to a friend sharing recent or hypothetical cultural experiences. The letter should describe a "breathtaking" or "unforgettable" adventure, mentioning "traditions" or "diversity." Students must include at least two sentences in the past continuous to narrate past experiences and one superlative.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Option C (Group Coordination Messages):</b> In small groups (3-4 students), write a series of 5-7 short chat messages (total 150-200 words) to coordinate logistics for a fictional group project focused on cultural exploration (e.g., planning a visit to a remote destination, researching traditional crafts). Messages should include confirming meeting times, discussing specific preferences for the trip, and assigning tasks, ensuring everyone has input. They must use second conditional for preferences (e.g., "If we could travel by train, I would prefer that") and at least three target vocabulary words like <i>destination, explore, meet, plan</i>. This task also integrates sociolinguistic competence by having students "lead group projects that explore cultural diversity and the personal benefits of travel, ensuring everyone has input."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">__/__ - __/__ <br> 31-40 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy. Students reflect on their learning and output.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher uses an observation checklist and a basic rubric to assess the written product from Stage 4. The rubric focuses on clarity, coherence, appropriate register, and the accurate use of target grammar and vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their chosen written task (formal letter, informal letter, or group messages).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Content & Task Completion:</b> Does the writing address all aspects of the prompt? Is it coherent and relevant to the theme?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar & Vocabulary:</b> Accurate use of Second Conditional, Superlatives, Past Continuous. Appropriate and varied use of target vocabulary (<i>diversity, adventure, enriching, breathtaking, traditions</i>, etc.).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Organization & Cohesion:</b> Clear structure, logical flow of ideas, appropriate formal/informal register.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Length Requirement:</b> Meets the specified word count (150-200 words).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following prompts on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of writing your letter/messages today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Name one new word or grammar structure you feel more confident using in writing now."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">__/__ - __/__ <br> 41-47 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Reviews the lesson, connects to future learning, and allows for teacher self-evaluation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did using the second conditional help you express your dream travel experiences?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you learn today about adapting your writing style for formal versus informal communication?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What aspects of the lesson worked particularly well, especially in engaging students with the writing tasks? Were the instructions for formal/informal writing clear for 12th graders? What adjustments or additional scaffolds might be needed for students who struggled with grammatical accuracy or vocabulary integration in their writing?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The written products from this lesson (letters/messages about cultural experiences and travel planning) will serve as input for Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration, where students will consolidate and summarize information from their written pieces to prepare for a 21st Century Skills Project (e.g., presenting their dream trip or cultural exchange program to the class).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">__/__ - __/__ <br> 48-50 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>